@endmetadata

# Code, maths and diagrams

## Code

@raw
@code(language="python", filename="train.py")
def train(model, data):
    return model.fit(data)
@endcode
@endraw

@table
Argument | Meaning
---------|--------
`language` | Used for syntax highlighting, and by editors to highlight the block inline
`filename` | Shown as a label
`caption` | Shown beneath
@endtable

A `@code` block with no `language` is a warning: highlighting cannot be applied
and a reader cannot tell what it is looking at.

The payload is taken literally. Nothing inside is parsed as XTXT, so a code
sample can contain `@` characters freely.

@note
Title: One exception, and it matters
Because blocks do not nest, a `@code` block cannot contain `@endcode` — the
first one closes it. Documenting XTXT in XTXT runs into this immediately; the
examples on this page use `@raw` for exactly that reason. See Limitations.
@endnote

## Maths

@code(language="text")
@math(caption="Mass–energy equivalence")
E = mc^2
@endmath
@endcode

The payload is passed through untouched. XTXT does not specify a maths syntax —
a renderer is free to hand it to KaTeX, MathJax or nothing at all.

## Diagrams

@code(language="text")
@mermaid(caption="Parse pipeline")
graph LR
  A[source] --> B[parse] --> C[AST] --> D[render]
@endmermaid
@endcode

Rendered as a `<pre class="mermaid">` block. Diagrams only draw when the page
loads Mermaid, which is opt-in:

@code(language="shell")
xtxt export notes.xtxt html --mermaid
@endcode

Off by default so exported output stays self-contained and works offline. With
the flag, the loader comes from a CDN — which means the page needs a network to
draw its diagrams. That trade is yours to make per export.
